# Build output
/bin/

# A cross-compile check (`GOOS=windows go build ./cmd/plock`) drops the binary in the
# tree next to the package it built. It is not an artefact anybody wants tracked.
*.exe

# The same binary for the host: `go build ./cmd/plock` with no -o writes ./plock in the
# module root, and that is exactly what a mutation-proofing step runs. `*.exe` covers the
# cross-compiled name and nothing covered this one, so a `git add -A` swept a 10 MB blob
# onto four separate branches. An ignore rule does not untrack a file that is already
# tracked -- `git rm --cached plock` does that -- but it stops the next one.
/plock

# Test and coverage artefacts
*.test
*.out
coverage.*

# Local agent state (Magic Context session/note storage). Never part of the
# project; it was swept in by `git add -A` twice, which is why it is ignored rather
# than remembered.
.cortexkit/

# Editor noise
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Local agent state (Magic Context); never part of the project
.cortexkit/
